  "answer": "The largest planet in the solar system is Jupiter. Here’s a long explanation for each point:\n- Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system, with a diameter of about 86,881 miles (139,822 kilometers). This is significantly larger than the next largest planet, Saturn, which has a diameter of about 72,367 miles (116,460 kilometers).\n- Jupiter's mass is also much greater than any other planet, approximately 1.898 × 10^27 kg, which is about 317.83 times the mass of Earth. Its mass is so large that it is classified as a gas giant, along with Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.\n- Jupiter's composition is primarily hydrogen and helium, similar to the Sun, which indicates that it formed early in the solar system's history, closer to the Sun than its current position. This makes it one of the oldest planets in the solar system.",
